1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,931
Welcome to the final chapter of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:05,931 --> 00:00:07,888
There's no new model code here.

3
00:00:07,888 --> 00:00:18,431
Instead we look back at what we built, talk about building responsibly, and map out three paths and three concrete capstone projects for the next six to twelve months.

4
00:00:18,431 --> 00:00:26,575
I'll also share what this series taught me from running every notebook on a plain CPU, including the bugs we found along the way.

5
00:00:27,425 --> 00:00:31,438
We've gone from what is AI all the way to a deployed API.

6
00:00:31,438 --> 00:00:35,029
Today we consolidate and choose what to build next.

7
00:00:35,029 --> 00:00:41,718
The chapter eighteen notebook is a skills checklist, a learning plan and two starter templates.

8
00:00:42,568 --> 00:00:43,198
The plan.

9
00:00:43,198 --> 00:00:45,017
The journey in five parts.

10
00:00:45,017 --> 00:00:46,346
A skills checklist.

11
00:00:46,346 --> 00:00:47,816
Responsible building.

12
00:00:47,816 --> 00:00:48,655
Three paths.

13
00:00:48,655 --> 00:00:49,775
Three capstones.

14
00:00:49,775 --> 00:00:52,294
Communities and how to read a paper.

15
00:00:52,294 --> 00:00:54,533
My own lessons from this series.

16
00:00:54,533 --> 00:00:56,072
And a final checklist.

17
00:00:56,922 --> 00:00:58,709
Some terms for the road.

18
00:00:58,709 --> 00:01:02,505
MLOps and LLMOps mean running models in production.

19
00:01:02,505 --> 00:01:05,780
Quantization stores weights with fewer bits.

20
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:09,353
Structured outputs force JSON or function calls.

21
00:01:09,353 --> 00:01:12,330
Sparse attention skips most token pairs.

22
00:01:12,330 --> 00:01:16,647
Mixture of experts routes each token to a few specialists.

23
00:01:16,647 --> 00:01:20,816
And interpretability asks why the model did what it did.

24
00:01:21,665 --> 00:01:22,271
Part one.

25
00:01:22,271 --> 00:01:23,884
Look how far we've come.

26
00:01:24,734 --> 00:01:27,767
Here's the journey, redrawn in five parts.

27
00:01:27,767 --> 00:01:30,511
Understanding in chapters one to four.

28
00:01:30,511 --> 00:01:33,038
Python and tensors in five and six.

29
00:01:33,038 --> 00:01:39,393
Data, tokenizer, embeddings, attention, the transformer and training in seven to twelve.

30
00:01:39,393 --> 00:01:43,942
Fine tuning, prompting and applications in thirteen to fifteen.

31
00:01:43,942 --> 00:01:47,625
Production and deployment in sixteen and seventeen.

32
00:01:48,475 --> 00:01:50,440
Part one gave us the why.

33
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:51,933
Part two the tools.

34
00:01:51,933 --> 00:01:56,807
Part three the model itself, built piece by piece and trained.

35
00:01:56,807 --> 00:02:02,073
Part four made it useful with LoRA, prompting, retrieval and tools.

36
00:02:02,073 --> 00:02:07,653
And part five shipped it behind a validated, logged API on a cloud GPU.

37
00:02:08,504 --> 00:02:12,788
The checklist, in three columns: theory, building and production.

38
00:02:12,788 --> 00:02:18,522
The notebook starts you at zero out of fourteen and asks you to flip each one honestly.

39
00:02:18,522 --> 00:02:21,883
If you followed the exercises, most should be true.

40
00:02:21,883 --> 00:02:25,442
If one isn't, that tells you which chapter to revisit.

41
00:02:26,292 --> 00:02:27,861
These aren't toy skills.

42
00:02:27,861 --> 00:02:31,978
Frontier labs use the same fundamentals at a much bigger scale.

43
00:02:31,978 --> 00:02:35,050
And you understand them because you built them.

44
00:02:35,050 --> 00:02:38,644
Sometimes you fixed them too, as we'll see in a moment.

45
00:02:39,494 --> 00:02:40,147
Part two.

46
00:02:40,147 --> 00:02:41,670
Building responsibly.

47
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:46,967
The author frames responsibility as good engineering, and I agree.

48
00:02:46,967 --> 00:02:52,358
Asking what could go wrong before you deploy is the same habit as writing tests.

49
00:02:52,358 --> 00:02:57,816
There are three areas to question: your data, your outputs and your transparency.

50
00:02:58,666 --> 00:03:00,267
Here they are as a grid.

51
00:03:00,267 --> 00:03:05,336
For data: where did it come from, is it licensed, what biases does it carry.

52
00:03:05,336 --> 00:03:12,674
For outputs: could it cause harm, what if someone believes a hallucination, are you validating and moderating.

53
00:03:12,674 --> 00:03:18,144
For transparency: do users know it's AI, are you logging, can you trace a failure.

54
00:03:18,994 --> 00:03:21,283
We practised these along the way.

55
00:03:21,283 --> 00:03:23,433
Data sourcing in chapter seven.

56
00:03:23,433 --> 00:03:24,959
Guardrails in fifteen.

57
00:03:24,959 --> 00:03:28,219
Validation, logging and rate limits in sixteen.

58
00:03:28,219 --> 00:03:30,300
Secrets and keys in seventeen.

59
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:35,710
The book says ethics is a practice you maintain, not a checklist you complete.

60
00:03:35,710 --> 00:03:43,271
One small correction: it credits chapter seventeen with audit trails, but logging was really chapter sixteen.

61
00:03:44,121 --> 00:03:47,411
Whatever you build next will fail sometimes.

62
00:03:47,411 --> 00:03:50,252
Retrieval will grab the wrong passage.

63
00:03:50,252 --> 00:03:52,420
Fine tunes will inherit bias.

64
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:54,813
Agents will call the wrong tool.

65
00:03:54,813 --> 00:04:04,159
So set expectations, add guardrails, log everything, test on diverse inputs and let users report problems or override the AI.

66
00:04:04,159 --> 00:04:07,673
The goal is failing gracefully, not perfection.

67
00:04:08,523 --> 00:04:09,163
Part three.

68
00:04:09,163 --> 00:04:10,443
Where to go from here.

69
00:04:11,293 --> 00:04:14,586
Three directions for the next six to twelve months.

70
00:04:14,586 --> 00:04:16,523
Research, builder and systems.

71
00:04:16,523 --> 00:04:21,042
It's not a permanent career choice, and many people move between them.

72
00:04:21,892 --> 00:04:23,321
Path one is research.

73
00:04:23,321 --> 00:04:33,797
Learn evaluation beyond loss and perplexity, architecture variants like sparse attention, mixture of experts and state space models, and interpretability.

74
00:04:33,797 --> 00:04:39,444
The book says this builds on chapter six, but six is the NumPy and PyTorch chapter.

75
00:04:39,444 --> 00:04:43,798
Training is chapter twelve and evaluation is in chapter fifteen.

76
00:04:43,798 --> 00:04:48,900
Start with the original transformer paper, which you can now actually read.

77
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:51,475
Path two is the builder.

78
00:04:51,475 --> 00:05:01,822
Reliable JSON and function calls, better retrieval with hybrid search and re ranking, and the user experience of streaming and graceful failure.

79
00:05:01,822 --> 00:05:06,564
The book points to chapter fourteen for RAG and fifteen for tools.

80
00:05:06,564 --> 00:05:12,385
In fact, both RAG and tool use are in chapter fifteen, and fourteen is prompting.

81
00:05:13,235 --> 00:05:14,767
Path three is systems.

82
00:05:14,767 --> 00:05:19,017
Make inference cheap with quantization, caching and batching.

83
00:05:19,017 --> 00:05:22,291
Make it observable with dashboards and tracing.

84
00:05:22,291 --> 00:05:28,142
And run it like software with prompt versioning, A B tests and evaluation pipelines.

85
00:05:28,142 --> 00:05:31,347
This builds on chapters sixteen and seventeen.

86
00:05:32,197 --> 00:05:37,631
Real projects need all three: technical depth, product sense and production skill.

87
00:05:37,631 --> 00:05:41,541
Pick one to go deep on and stay competent in the other two.

88
00:05:42,391 --> 00:05:43,064
Part four.

89
00:05:43,064 --> 00:05:45,015
Stop reading, start building.

90
00:05:45,865 --> 00:05:50,806
The book offers three capstones, each sized for a few weekends.

91
00:05:50,806 --> 00:05:52,296
RAG with citations.

92
00:05:52,296 --> 00:05:54,649
A fine tuned domain assistant.

93
00:05:54,649 --> 00:05:56,532
And a single tool agent.

94
00:05:57,382 --> 00:06:01,322
Capstone one: a RAG system over your own documents.

95
00:06:01,322 --> 00:06:15,307
Collect a corpus, chunk and embed it using chapter fifteen's RAG section, which the book labels chapter fourteen, return sources with every answer, wrap it in the API and deploy it.

96
00:06:15,307 --> 00:06:18,089
Then grade twenty questions by hand.

97
00:06:18,089 --> 00:06:21,488
The stretch goal is hybrid search with BM25.

98
00:06:22,338 --> 00:06:24,825
Capstone two: a domain assistant.

99
00:06:24,825 --> 00:06:40,123
Curate five hundred to a thousand clean examples, QLoRA tune a seven billion model using chapter thirteen's ideas with the PEFT library, test on twenty questions the base model fails, compare and deploy.

100
00:06:40,123 --> 00:06:45,549
The stretch is DPO, which learns from preferred versus rejected answers.

101
00:06:46,399 --> 00:06:49,626
Capstone three: an agent with exactly one tool.

102
00:06:49,626 --> 00:06:56,217
Define its schema, decide with structured outputs, and test twenty cases, half needing the tool.

103
00:06:56,217 --> 00:06:58,345
Aim for nineteen out of twenty.

104
00:06:58,345 --> 00:07:04,593
One warning from the notebook's own starter: its calculator uses eval with empty built ins.

105
00:07:04,593 --> 00:07:08,095
That still isn't safe, so use a proper math parser.

106
00:07:08,945 --> 00:07:09,904
How to pick.

107
00:07:09,904 --> 00:07:12,622
To consolidate, do the RAG system.

108
00:07:12,622 --> 00:07:15,739
To learn something new, do fine tuning.

109
00:07:15,739 --> 00:07:17,737
For fun, build the agent.

110
00:07:17,737 --> 00:07:21,254
The only wrong choice is analysis paralysis.

111
00:07:22,104 --> 00:07:25,121
Here's a plan I'd use for any of the three.

112
00:07:25,121 --> 00:07:30,664
Weekend one: data and a twenty question test set, so you have a baseline score.

113
00:07:30,664 --> 00:07:34,382
Weekend two: the core pipeline, beating the baseline.

114
00:07:34,382 --> 00:07:37,680
Weekend three: the API, logging and guardrails.

115
00:07:37,680 --> 00:07:41,539
Weekend four: deploy and write it up with real numbers.

116
00:07:42,389 --> 00:07:45,352
Measure it the way chapter fifteen taught.

117
00:07:45,352 --> 00:07:47,751
Freeze the test set before tuning.

118
00:07:47,751 --> 00:07:53,254
Report accuracy, citation correctness, latency and cost per thousand requests.

119
00:07:53,254 --> 00:07:56,217
Keep a log of every failure and its cause.

120
00:07:56,217 --> 00:08:00,309
And always compare against a baseline without the feature.

121
00:08:01,159 --> 00:08:02,893
Make it portfolio ready.

122
00:08:02,893 --> 00:08:07,950
A README that goes from problem to approach to numbers to limitations.

123
00:08:07,950 --> 00:08:09,323
A short demo video.

124
00:08:09,323 --> 00:08:11,418
A live URL with a budget cap.

125
00:08:11,418 --> 00:08:17,415
And an honest section on what didn't work, which is often the most impressive part.

126
00:08:18,265 --> 00:08:18,894
Part five.

127
00:08:18,894 --> 00:08:20,782
You don't have to learn alone.

128
00:08:21,632 --> 00:08:23,729
Resources by comfort level.

129
00:08:23,729 --> 00:08:28,775
Start with the Hugging Face course and the local LLaMA community.

130
00:08:28,775 --> 00:08:33,745
When you're confident, Stanford's CS324 and the original papers.

131
00:08:33,745 --> 00:08:43,605
Then join the Hugging Face Discord or MLOps community, and contribute small things to open source: docs, bug reports, examples.

132
00:08:44,456 --> 00:08:47,136
The book's method for reading papers.

133
00:08:47,136 --> 00:08:49,309
Abstract and conclusion first.

134
00:08:49,309 --> 00:08:50,541
Then the figures.

135
00:08:50,541 --> 00:08:52,714
Skip the maths the first time.

136
00:08:52,714 --> 00:08:56,046
Focus on what the experiments actually tested.

137
00:08:56,046 --> 00:08:58,002
And read related work last.

138
00:08:58,852 --> 00:08:59,469
Part six.

139
00:08:59,469 --> 00:09:02,074
What running every notebook taught me.

140
00:09:02,923 --> 00:09:07,076
My biggest lesson: run the code and read your own logs.

141
00:09:07,076 --> 00:09:15,305
In chapter eleven, loaded GPT-2 wrote foxes es es es, because of missing attention biases and the wrong GELU.

142
00:09:15,305 --> 00:09:21,043
In chapter twelve, the loss ignored the exclamation mark instead of padding.

143
00:09:21,043 --> 00:09:25,724
In thirteen, the LoRA counts and naming didn't match the code.

144
00:09:25,724 --> 00:09:29,046
In seventeen, a T4 can't run bfloat sixteen.

145
00:09:29,046 --> 00:09:33,425
Every one of these is in the errata file in my repository.

146
00:09:33,425 --> 00:09:36,143
None of it takes away from the book.

147
00:09:36,143 --> 00:09:40,144
Finding these is how I actually learned the material.

148
00:09:40,994 --> 00:09:43,312
Here's where our own model sits.

149
00:09:43,312 --> 00:09:52,222
Our sixteen million parameter MiniGPT, trained on a third of a million Shakespeare tokens, writes Shakespeare shaped noise.

150
00:09:52,222 --> 00:09:57,510
GPT-2 small, which we loaded in chapter eleven, is fluent but unreliable.

151
00:09:57,510 --> 00:10:08,447
Llama three point two, three billion parameters, which we ran locally through Ollama for chapters fourteen to sixteen, is a genuinely useful assistant.

152
00:10:08,447 --> 00:10:11,128
It's the same family of architecture.

153
00:10:11,128 --> 00:10:14,097
The difference is scale, data and tuning.

154
00:10:14,947 --> 00:10:16,668
The series by the numbers.

155
00:10:16,668 --> 00:10:20,969
Eighteen chapters, and fourteen notebooks run on an ordinary CPU.

156
00:10:20,969 --> 00:10:25,733
The first seventeen videos add up to about five hours and fifty minutes.

157
00:10:25,733 --> 00:10:30,827
Along the way I logged sixty seven errata, every one checked by running code.

158
00:10:30,827 --> 00:10:40,355
That's not a criticism of the book: checking a book against running code is the best way to learn from it, and I'd encourage you to do the same.

159
00:10:41,206 --> 00:10:43,112
What I'd do next myself.

160
00:10:43,112 --> 00:10:51,929
Retrain MiniGPT with the chapter twelve fixes on a bigger corpus such as TinyStories, for about an hour of GPU.

161
00:10:51,929 --> 00:10:57,568
LoRA tune Llama three point two on my own FAQ and evaluate it properly.

162
00:10:57,568 --> 00:11:01,302
And deploy it on an L4 with a small budget cap.

163
00:11:02,152 --> 00:11:04,872
My answers to the review questions.

164
00:11:04,872 --> 00:11:08,758
The three paths are research, builder and systems.

165
00:11:08,758 --> 00:11:14,044
Responsibility is ongoing because data, users and models all change.

166
00:11:14,044 --> 00:11:16,376
My capstone is the RAG system.

167
00:11:16,376 --> 00:11:20,884
My next reads are the LoRA paper and vLLM's documentation.

168
00:11:20,884 --> 00:11:26,791
And what surprised me most is how much behaviour hides in a single argument.

169
00:11:27,642 --> 00:11:37,582
Before you close the book: score the fourteen skills, choose one capstone and start this week, bookmark one resource, and share your deployed URL.

170
00:11:37,582 --> 00:11:40,101
The author ends with a personal note.

171
00:11:40,101 --> 00:11:48,612
He's a backend developer who wrote the book to teach himself how LLMs work, and his closing line is "now go build something".

172
00:11:49,462 --> 00:11:53,881
This series is based on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

173
00:11:53,881 --> 00:12:04,331
The explanations and diagrams are my own, but the book is the real thing, so please support the author and buy it at leanpub dot com slash First LLM.

174
00:12:04,331 --> 00:12:09,171
The code is on GitHub at First LLM slash code, under the MIT licence.

175
00:12:09,171 --> 00:12:14,782
All my slides, notes and the errata for the series are in my Courses repository.

176
00:12:14,782 --> 00:12:17,588
Thank you for watching the whole series.

177
00:12:17,588 --> 00:12:19,201
Now go build something.

